# Benchmarks

These benchmarks measure the throughput and latency cost of the tunnel itself — encryption, framing, and multiplexing — and how each transport holds up under packet loss. The goal is to provide enough data to choose the right transport for the environment.

All results use `iperf3` pushing traffic through a full end-to-end tunnel with both nodes running and encryption active. Results are from v0.1.2 on Linux x86_64. To reproduce: `just benchmark` in the `bench/` directory.

## Loopback

The entry and exit nodes are on the same machine with no real network between them. This isolates the cost of the tunnel itself — encryption, framing, and multiplexing — from any network conditions.

### Throughput

| Transport | 1 stream | 4 streams | Reverse |
|-----------|----------|-----------|---------|
| UDP (QUIC) | 2.4 Gbps | 2.4 Gbps | 581 Mbps |
| TCP (WebSockets) | 2.4 Gbps | 2.5 Gbps | — |

Both transports are equivalent when there is no packet loss.

### Latency

| Transport | Avg RTT | Jitter |
|-----------|---------|--------|
| UDP (QUIC) | 0.164 ms | 0.034 ms |
| TCP (WebSockets) | 0.129 ms | 0.034 ms |

WebSockets is 21% lower latency. QUIC runs TLS and stream framing in userspace, adding overhead that doesn't pay off without packet loss.

## Synthetic packet loss

The same loopback setup with [netem](https://wiki.linuxfoundation.org/networking/netem) (the Linux network emulation tool) injecting packet loss and added delay on the tunnel link, simulating packet loss and latency on the tunnel link.

### Throughput (4 parallel streams)

| Transport | 0.5% loss / +10ms RTT | 2.0% loss / +50ms RTT |
|-----------|----------------------|----------------------|
| UDP (QUIC) | 58 Mbps | 17 Mbps |
| TCP (WebSockets) | 89 Mbps | 7.7 Mbps |

At low loss WebSockets still leads. At 2% loss QUIC pulls ahead — each connection gets an independent stream, so a dropped packet stalls only that stream rather than everything sharing the same TCP flow.
